# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) for local development, and set the same two
# variables in the hosting provider's dashboard for a deployment.
#
# Both are `VITE_`-prefixed, which means they are INLINED INTO THE CLIENT BUNDLE
# at build time and are readable by anyone who views source. That is fine for
# these two values and only because row-level security is on — the policies in
# supabase/migrations/ are what protect the data, never the key.
#
# Two consequences that have both bitten this project:
#
#   1. Changing a value does nothing to an existing build. A running dev server
#      or a deployed bundle keeps the value it was built with. Rebuild.
#   2. Putting the WRONG KIND of key here publishes it. See below.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# ⚠️  THIS MUST BE THE PUBLISHABLE KEY.
#
# Supabase issues two keys that sit next to each other in the dashboard and look
# alike:
#
#     sb_publishable_...   safe to publish. Subject to RLS. ← use this one
#     sb_secret_...        BYPASSES RLS ENTIRELY. Server-side only. Never here.
#
# (On older projects the pair is a JWT `anon` key and a JWT `service_role` key.
# Use `anon`. They differ only in a base64 payload nobody decodes by eye.)
#
# On 2026-08-05 this variable was set to a `sb_secret_` key and shipped into a
# world-readable bundle. Nothing failed, because nothing can: a secret key makes
# every request succeed while every policy is void. The build now refuses to
# continue if it sees one — see src/lib/keyShape.js and docs/access-model.md.
#
# If a secret key is ever built, ROTATE IT at the provider. Fixing this variable
# and redeploying does not invalidate what was already published.
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_your_publishable_key_here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Calendar — story #95. OPTIONAL: without it the app runs, the roster
# still offers "Connect Google Calendar" to a member with a real address, and
# pressing it says the variable is not set rather than doing nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ⚠️  THIS MUST BE THE CLIENT ID, NOT THE CLIENT SECRET.
#
# The same trap as the key above, one console over. Google shows both on one
# screen, a few lines apart, and they get pasted in the same sitting:
#
#     ...apps.googleusercontent.com   the client ID. Public by design — it is in
#                                     the consent URL, which is an address bar.
#     GOCSPX-...                      the client SECRET. Never here. It belongs
#                                     to the Edge Function's environment, set
#                                     with `npx supabase secrets set`.
#
# Putting the secret here produces a WORKING BUILD that publishes a credential
# able to mint access to every connected calendar. The build refuses if it sees
# one — src/lib/keyShape.js, extended for Google by #95 rather than duplicated —
# and the refusal names the Google console rather than the Supabase one, because
# a message pointing at the wrong dashboard sends somebody to rotate nothing.
#
# Where it comes from, and the redirect URIs that have to be registered
# alongside it, are in docs/deploy-runbook.md section 3b.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The live RLS suite only — `npm run test:rls`. Not read by the app, not read by
# CI, and NOT `VITE_`-prefixed, so neither is ever built into a bundle.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A pre-confirmed Supabase account for the suite to sign in as. It needs one
# because it cannot make one: this project has `mailer_autoconfirm: false`, so a
# client `signUp()` returns a null session until somebody clicks a link in an
# inbox, and no automated suite can read an inbox. (Measured 2026-08-21 against
# `GET /auth/v1/settings`. `src/lib/household.js` says the same thing about the
# app's own signup path.)
#
# Make one ONCE, in the Supabase dashboard:
#
#     Authentication -> Users -> Add user -> Create new user
#     tick "Auto Confirm User"
#
# Use a throwaway address that belongs to NOBODY IN THE HOUSEHOLD. This account
# becomes the organizer of every TEST household the suite creates, and there is
# deliberately no client-reachable way to delete a household, so it accumulates
# them. Pointing it at a real person's account would bury their own household in
# test data.
TASKR_TEST_EMAIL=suite@your-throwaway-domain.example
TASKR_TEST_PASSWORD=a-long-throwaway-password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `npm run migrate:live` and `npm run probe:live-grants` only — story #185.
# OPTIONAL: without it the app runs, CI runs, and every other command runs. The
# two above refuse, by name, rather than falling back to anything.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ⚠️  THIS IS THE MOST DANGEROUS VALUE IN THIS FILE.
#
# Every other credential here is scoped to one project, and the two above it are
# public by design. A Supabase PERSONAL ACCESS TOKEN is neither:
#
#     it authenticates as YOU, not as a project
#     it has full authority over EVERY project in the account
#     it can create, pause and DELETE projects, not merely read and write rows
#
# There is no row-level security in front of it and no policy that limits it. It
# is the first long-lived, account-wide secret this repo has ever needed, and the
# reason #185 was filed as its own story rather than folded into #150.
#
# WHERE IT COMES FROM — the ACCOUNT page, not the project page. That distinction
# is the whole of the confusion this file keeps warning about:
#
#     https://supabase.com/dashboard/account/tokens
#
# The value is shown ONCE, at creation. Supabase cannot show it again.
#
# HOW TO REVOKE IT — same page, "Revoke" beside the token. Do that:
#
#   - the moment it has been used for whatever it was minted for, if that was a
#     one-off. Revoking is free and minting another takes ten seconds;
#   - immediately, if it has been pasted anywhere that is not this file — a
#     terminal that scrolls into a transcript, a chat window, a commit, a CI log;
#   - immediately, if you are unsure. There is no partial exposure of a token
#     with this authority, and rotating costs nothing.
#
# Revoking takes effect at once and breaks nothing except the two commands above.
#
# IT IS NEVER COMMITTED. `.gitignore` excludes `.env*` except this example, and
# `src/test/gate.test.js` scans every file in the repo — tracked and untracked —
# for a token-shaped literal and fails the build if it finds one. That scan is
# why the placeholder below is written with underscores: a real token has none
# after its prefix, so the scan can tell a placeholder from a credential without
# an allowlist for this file. KEEP THE UNDERSCORES if you change it.
SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=sbp_your_personal_access_token
